# Cloudflare Pages redirects for the React rebuild. Shipped as dist/_redirects.
# Tests: app/tests/redirects.test.ts (fixture: app/tests/fixtures/redirect-tables.json).
# Live check: node scripts/crawl-check.mjs
#
# Host rules (http to https, www to apex) are NOT here. Pages _redirects cannot
# match a host, so they stay a Cloudflare zone Redirect Rule. Do not add them back.
#
# How Pages resolves our files (dist has dallas.html, blog.html, blog/<slug>.html,
# case-studies.html, case-studies/<slug>.html and an admin/index.html):
#   Order: _redirects first (first match wins), then the file lookup below.
#   /dallas              serves dallas.html, 200
#   /dallas/             no dallas/index.html, but dallas.html exists: Pages 308s to /dallas
#   /dallas.html         hits the global /*.html rule below: 301 to /dallas
#   /blog                serves blog.html. The dist/blog/ folder does not matter: Pages
#                        tries the exact file, then blog.html, then blog/index.html
#   /blog/               no blog/index.html, blog.html exists: Pages 308s to /blog
#   /blog/<slug>         serves blog/<slug>.html
#   /blog/<slug>/        no blog/<slug>/index.html: Pages 308s to /blog/<slug>
#   /case-studies and /case-studies/<slug> behave exactly like /blog and /blog/<slug>
#                        (the case-studies/<slug>/ folders hold only images)
#   /admin               no admin.html, admin/index.html exists: Pages 308s to /admin/
#   /admin/              serves admin/index.html (Decap CMS)
# So there is no global "/:path/ /:path" rule. It was the cause of the live /admin/
# loop (301 to /admin, then Pages 308 back to /admin/) and Pages already does it.
#
# Rules for legacy URLs come in three variants (bare, .html, trailing slash) and
# sit ABOVE /*.html, so each reaches its final page in ONE hop. A legacy .html
# URL that fell through to the global rule would hop twice.
# /*.html is the only dynamic rule (Pages allows 100 dynamic, 2000 static rules).
# It cannot break real assets: nothing in dist is served as an .html file URL
# except admin/index.html, which has its own rule above it.
# Paths that are real pages are never listed as a source. /privacy-wall is a
# page now, so its old 301 to /residential is gone.
# Gone URLs (410) are not here: functions/[[path]].js answers them.

# Index and CMS entry points (the global rule below would turn these into /index and /admin/index)
